1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,767
Last time, three ideal windings made a
smooth, rotating field.

2
00:00:04,807 --> 00:00:08,069
But real copper can't be spread smoothly
around the bore.

3
00:00:08,109 --> 00:00:11,763
In this motor, it's twelve coils, one
around each tooth.

4
00:00:11,803 --> 00:00:14,525
So the field it makes comes out as a
staircase.

5
00:00:14,565 --> 00:00:17,547
Today we'll compute those steps from the
winding layout,

6
00:00:17,587 --> 00:00:21,702
compare them with a sine wave, and find
the part that makes torque.

7
00:00:23,002 --> 00:00:25,538
First, last lesson's three predictions.

8
00:00:25,578 --> 00:00:29,704
One: six amps at three centimetres gives
forty microtesla.

9
00:00:29,744 --> 00:00:36,071
Two: on the axis of a coil, the field
halves at about 0.77 times the radius.

10
00:00:36,111 --> 00:00:37,730
Three: swap two phase leads,

11
00:00:37,770 --> 00:00:41,693
and the rotating field reverses direction,
at the same speed.

12
00:00:41,733 --> 00:00:44,932
The current peaks now reach the windings
in the order a,

13
00:00:44,972 --> 00:00:49,174
then c, then b. Phase sequence and winding
layout have to agree.

14
00:00:50,474 --> 00:00:53,783
Here's the stator in cross-section. Draw a
closed path that

15
00:00:53,823 --> 00:00:57,361
crosses the air gap twice and returns
through the steel.

16
00:00:57,401 --> 00:01:02,461
Ampère's law: the line integral of H
around it equals the current it encloses.

17
00:01:02,501 --> 00:01:05,732
The steel is so permeable that it needs
almost no H.

18
00:01:05,772 --> 00:01:10,410
Only the two gap crossings count: each is
H times the gap length, g.

19
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:13,926
Call that product F, the magnetomotive
force, or MMF.

20
00:01:13,966 --> 00:01:19,367
F at the second crossing, minus F at the
first, equals the enclosed current.

21
00:01:19,407 --> 00:01:21,724
Now sweep the second crossing around.

22
00:01:21,764 --> 00:01:27,129
F stays flat until the path passes a slot,
then jumps by that slot's current.

23
00:01:27,169 --> 00:01:31,414
That assumes ideal steel, a uniform gap,
and each slot's current

24
00:01:31,454 --> 00:01:35,971
concentrated at its centre: no slot
openings, no saturation.

25
00:01:37,271 --> 00:01:39,513
Let's unroll the gap into a straight line,

26
00:01:39,553 --> 00:01:42,661
with the stator on top and the rotor
underneath.

27
00:01:42,761 --> 00:01:45,507
One coil of N turns, carrying current i,

28
00:01:45,547 --> 00:01:50,692
makes F jump up by N i at one coil side,
and back down at the other.

29
00:01:50,732 --> 00:01:56,570
Ampère's law only fixes differences. The
level comes from flux: every line leaving

30
00:01:56,610 --> 00:02:02,683
the rotor returns to it, so the gap flux
density, μ₀F over g, averages to zero.

31
00:02:02,723 --> 00:02:06,110
So one coil gives a zero-mean square wave:

32
00:02:06,150 --> 00:02:11,465
11/12 of N i over its own tooth, and −1/12
everywhere else.

33
00:02:12,765 --> 00:02:16,204
This motor's layout is a, b, c, repeated
four times,

34
00:02:16,244 --> 00:02:19,281
with every coil spanning a single tooth.

35
00:02:19,321 --> 00:02:23,896
Phase a takes teeth 1, 4, 7 and 10, in
series. Their square waves

36
00:02:23,936 --> 00:02:29,245
add into four positive blocks: four pole
pairs, for the 8-pole rotor.

37
00:02:29,285 --> 00:02:33,216
That's a concentrated winding. Slots per
pole per phase,

38
00:02:33,256 --> 00:02:36,431
q, is 12 over 8 times 3: just one half.

39
00:02:36,471 --> 00:02:41,425
A distributed winding spreads each phase
over a group of adjacent slots,

40
00:02:41,465 --> 00:02:47,165
a phase belt. With 24 slots and 4 poles, q
is 2, and the steps come in pairs.

41
00:02:47,205 --> 00:02:50,369
More, smaller steps follow a sine wave
more closely,

42
00:02:50,409 --> 00:02:53,744
at the price of longer, overlapping end
turns.

43
00:02:55,044 --> 00:02:57,949
Now feed all three phases with balanced
currents.

44
00:02:57,989 --> 00:03:03,361
At this instant, phase a is at its peak,
and b and c each carry minus half of it.

45
00:03:03,401 --> 00:03:08,178
Each slot's current is the sum of its two
coil sides, and the staircase follows.

46
00:03:08,218 --> 00:03:12,362
Over each tooth, F is simply N times the
current in that tooth's coil.

47
00:03:12,402 --> 00:03:16,673
A Fourier fit gives the fundamental: one
and a half times one phase's

48
00:03:16,713 --> 00:03:20,649
— the three-halves from last time, now
from real slots.

49
00:03:21,957 --> 00:03:23,507
Now let time run.

50
00:03:23,607 --> 00:03:27,623
The staircase doesn't glide. It changes
shape, step by step.

51
00:03:27,823 --> 00:03:32,317
But its fundamental keeps a constant
amplitude, and travels steadily.

52
00:03:32,417 --> 00:03:35,473
One electrical cycle moves it by one pole
pair,

53
00:03:35,513 --> 00:03:39,200
a quarter turn here: the field turns at ω
over p.

54
00:03:39,240 --> 00:03:43,826
That's exactly the speed the 8-pole rotor
turns at, to stay in step.

55
00:03:45,226 --> 00:03:49,342
Let's package this as one function per
phase. The turns function,

56
00:03:49,382 --> 00:03:54,282
n of θ, counts the turns of phase a passed
as you sweep around the gap.

57
00:03:54,322 --> 00:03:58,353
Subtract its average, and you get the
winding function, N of θ.

58
00:03:58,393 --> 00:04:02,396
Then the MMF is each winding function
times its phase current,

59
00:04:02,436 --> 00:04:07,541
summed over the phases. Geometry lives in
N of θ; time, in the currents.

60
00:04:07,581 --> 00:04:13,133
Its fundamental is 4 over π, times the
series turns, times a winding factor,

61
00:04:13,173 --> 00:04:18,881
over the number of poles. For this
winding, the winding factor is 0.866.

62
00:04:18,921 --> 00:04:22,774
The same function sets how much rotor flux
each phase links,

63
00:04:22,814 --> 00:04:27,336
so the winding factor scales both back-EMF
and torque per amp.

64
00:04:28,636 --> 00:04:32,021
What else is in the staircase? Take its
Fourier transform.

65
00:04:32,061 --> 00:04:37,772
The working harmonic is order 4, matching
the 4 pole pairs. Then come orders 8,

66
00:04:37,812 --> 00:04:43,900
16, 20 and 28, falling as one over the
order: order 8 is half the fundamental.

67
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:48,349
Order 8 travels backwards, order 16
forwards, each at its own speed.

68
00:04:48,389 --> 00:04:52,274
None keeps step with the rotor, so none
adds average torque;

69
00:04:52,314 --> 00:04:56,518
they add ripple, and losses in the magnets
and rotor iron.

70
00:04:56,558 --> 00:05:01,965
Nothing appears below order 4: the layout
repeats four times around the stator,

71
00:05:02,005 --> 00:05:06,896
the common factor of 12 slots and 4 pole
pairs. No sub-harmonics.

72
00:05:06,936 --> 00:05:11,308
With a 10-pole rotor, the same twelve
coils are connected differently.

73
00:05:11,348 --> 00:05:15,530
The pattern repeats only once, and an
order-1 sub-harmonic appears,

74
00:05:15,570 --> 00:05:18,465
about a third of the working harmonic.

75
00:05:19,765 --> 00:05:22,856
Your turn. Write the winding as a table:
for each slot,

76
00:05:22,896 --> 00:05:25,817
which phase, which direction, how many
turns.

77
00:05:25,857 --> 00:05:29,918
Multiply by the phase currents, take a
running total around the stator,

78
00:05:29,958 --> 00:05:32,860
and subtract the mean: that's the
staircase.

79
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:39,170
An FFT gives the harmonics. Check against
these numbers: winding factor 0.866,

80
00:05:39,210 --> 00:05:46,335
a fundamental of N times the peak current
times 0.827, and order 8 at half of that.

81
00:05:46,375 --> 00:05:48,369
Compared with a pure sine wave,

82
00:05:48,409 --> 00:05:52,976
the harmonics carry an RMS of 68 percent
of the fundamental's.

83
00:05:54,276 --> 00:05:57,497
The winding table also predicts
resistance:

84
00:05:57,537 --> 00:06:02,958
4 coils of 6 turns per phase is about 1.8
metres of 1-millimetre copper,

85
00:06:02,998 --> 00:06:06,517
or 39 milliohms; 40 with the connections.

86
00:06:06,557 --> 00:06:11,450
That's too small for a two-wire ohmmeter:
leads and clips can add as much again.

87
00:06:11,490 --> 00:06:16,094
So use four wires. A constant-current
supply drives 2 amps through two

88
00:06:16,134 --> 00:06:20,605
motor leads, and a separate voltmeter
senses right at the terminals.

89
00:06:20,645 --> 00:06:24,837
It draws almost no current, so lead
resistance drops out.

90
00:06:24,877 --> 00:06:30,146
In star, line to line is two phases in
series: expect about 160 millivolts.

91
00:06:30,186 --> 00:06:35,410
Measure all three pairs; they should agree
within about one percent.

92
00:06:35,450 --> 00:06:40,711
Then correct for temperature: copper rises
0.393 percent per kelvin,

93
00:06:40,751 --> 00:06:45,617
so divide by one plus α times the rise
above 20 degrees.

94
00:06:46,933 --> 00:06:48,871
So where does this model stop?

95
00:06:48,911 --> 00:06:51,280
Real steel needs some MMF of its own,

96
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:54,789
and saturates at high current: Lessons 3
and 4.

97
00:06:54,829 --> 00:06:58,581
Real slots have openings. Spreading each
step across the

98
00:06:58,621 --> 00:07:02,983
2-millimetre opening lowers the
fundamental by under one percent;

99
00:07:03,023 --> 00:07:07,225
openings also make the gap look longer.
That's Lesson 5.

100
00:07:07,265 --> 00:07:09,770
And surface magnets act as extra gap,

101
00:07:09,810 --> 00:07:14,644
so this MMF makes only tens of millitesla.
Next time shows why.

102
00:07:15,944 --> 00:07:18,224
Three predictions before next time.

103
00:07:18,264 --> 00:07:20,766
One: a coil of N turns carries current I.

104
00:07:20,806 --> 00:07:24,097
What's the total jump of its MMF square
wave?

105
00:07:25,397 --> 00:07:29,498
Two: with balanced three-phase currents,
how does the fundamental of

106
00:07:29,538 --> 00:07:34,119
the total MMF compare with the peak
fundamental of one phase alone?

107
00:07:35,419 --> 00:07:38,180
Three: double the air gap, with ideal
iron.

108
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:42,563
What happens to the air-gap flux density
from the same MMF?

109
00:07:43,863 --> 00:07:46,497
Next time: magnetic circuits — reluctance,

110
00:07:46,537 --> 00:07:50,029
the air gap, and the magnet as a source of
MMF.
